 "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent David Rouzer (R) is running."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 7th District leans Republican by about seven points, and David Rouzer is the incumbent seeking another term. He holds a large financial edge, roughly $2.3 million on hand compared with about $65,000 for Democrat Kimberly Hardy, and he secured his party's nomination in March. Outside spending has been negligible, a sign that both sides see the race as settled.\n\nThe main question is whether a favorable national climate for Democrats can narrow the gap. A single late-August poll, from a Democratic-leaning pollster, put Rouzer ahead 45 to 39 among likely voters. Rating outlets split between Solid Republican and Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Hardy roughly a one-in-seven chance. Hardy would need a substantial wave and far more resources to compete; the evidence points to a Rouzer win.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0898, "margin": -8.11}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0938, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0915, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1198, "margin": -8.2}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0905, "margin": -8.5}]}